        "create_time": 1761731105.018072,
        "update_time": 1761731116.328024,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Short answer: maybe — **you can’t erase an FRP lock by just adding a new Google or Samsung account** if the phone is already FRP-locked after a factory reset. FRP is tied to the *previous* Google/Samsung account that was on the phone. Below I’ll tell you exactly what to try depending on the phone’s current state and give a short message you can send to the donor to remove the device for you.\n\n---\n\n# Two scenarios & what to do\n\n**A. You can use the phone right now (you’re inside Android / Settings)**  \nIf the phone is unlocked and you can reach Settings, you *can* make it yours:\n\n1. Settings → **Accounts and backup** → **Accounts** → **Add account** → **Google** — sign in with your Google account.  \n2. (Optional) Add a Samsung account the same way (Add account → Samsung account).  \n3. Remove the donor’s accounts: Settings → Accounts → select the old Google and Samsung accounts → **Remove account**.  \n4. Now do a clean factory reset: Settings → **General management** (or **System**) → **Reset** → **Factory data reset** → **Reset**.  \n5. After the reset, when setup runs, sign in with *your* Google account — FRP will now be tied to your account.\n\n**B. The phone is FRP-locked and at the setup screen (asks for previous owner’s Google account)**  \nYou cannot add a new account to bypass this. Legitimate options:\n\n- **Ask the donor to remove the device from their Google account remotely** (fastest). They can sign into their Google account on a computer/phone, go to their account → Devices or Device activity, find the device, and remove/sign out that device. After they remove it, wait a few minutes and reboot the phone — then the setup may proceed without asking for the old credentials.\n\n- **Ask the donor to remove the Samsung account** (if there is one) by signing into Samsung’s account portal (Find My Mobile) and removing the device.\n\n- **If the donor can’t do that** (lost account, unreachable), contact Samsung support and explain the situation. They may require proof the device was legally transferred to you (donation paperwork, a signed note from the donor, ID, etc.). Policies vary by country/carrier and Samsung will decide whether they can help. Be prepared that they may refuse without solid proof of legal transfer.\n\n---\n\n# What to tell the donor (copy & paste)\n```\nHi — thank you for donating the phone. I’m stuck at the setup screen that asks for the Google account previously used on the device. Could you please remove the phone from your account so I can finish setup?\n\nFor Google: sign into your Google account on a computer/phone → go to your account settings → Devices or Device activity → find the device and choose “Sign out” / “Remove”.\n\nFor Samsung (if used): sign into your Samsung account (Find My Mobile) and remove the device from your account.\n\nPlease let me know when it’s removed — thanks!\n```\n\n---\n\n# Important warnings\n- **Do not use FRP bypass tools or unofficial “services”** — many are scams, can brick the device, or are illegal. I won’t help bypass FRP.  \n- If you’re the legitimate new owner and the donor is cooperative, the donor removing the device from their accounts is the cleanest solution.\n\n---\n\nIf you tell me whether the phone is currently showing an FRP lock message / setup screen or whether you can open Settings, I’ll give the specific step-by-step for that exact case (and if you want, I can draft a slightly more formal message/email to the donor)."
          ]
        },
